    <h2 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.5rem;">Statutory Role of Encumbrance Certificates in Real Estate Due Diligence</h2>
    <p>Under the <strong>Registration Act of 1908</strong> and the <strong>Transfer of Property Act of 1882</strong>, verifying historical title deeds and encumbrances is the foundational legal protocol for any property acquisition or bank financing in India. An Encumbrance Certificate (EC) serves as an official statutory ledger transcript issued by the Sub-Registrar Office (SRO) within whose territorial jurisdiction the property resides. It details all registered transactions—including sale deeds, bank mortgages, gift deeds, court attachments, partition deeds, and release deeds—executed over a designated search period (typically 13 to 30 years).</p>
    
    <h3 style="color: var(--primary); font-size: 1.25rem; margin-top: 25px;">Key Elements Inspected in an Official EC Search</h3>
    <ul style="padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>Ownership Continuity & Chain of Title:</strong> Unbroken chronological trace of title transfers from original grantor to current owner.</li>
        <li><strong>Lien and Mortgage Disclosures:</strong> Details of registered mortgages, MODTD records, and financial charges created under the SARFAESI Act.</li>
        <li><strong>Court Injunctions & Lis Pendens:</strong> Registration of judicial stays, probate suits, or litigation notices under Section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act.</li>
        <li><strong>Property Identification Matching:</strong> Exact survey numbers, plot extents, door numbers, ward numbers, and cardinal boundaries matching municipal tax records.</li>
    </ul>

